A||N||5||BloodFlower||::frowns:: My car is acting weird...||||9||
Z||000000||BloodFlower||02-17-2001||12:15 PM||||Okay, the other day, my Fiero, an 85 SE v6, started acting weird.  The battery light came on, and stayed on all day.  Later that nite, I went to go get food, and she died.  I had her jumped, and we made it home.  well, the next morning, she died on the way to get the mail.  I called my brother and he came over and took the battery out, and we went and had it tested.  It tested at 12, so they charged it a lil bits, and sent me on my way.  she started up fine after that, so we assumed it was just the battery.  The next morning on the way out, she died again.  she had started up fine, but whn i stopped to get the mail, i had to shut her off, and then she wouldnt start back up.  The lights,and radio came on, but when I went to crank her, she just made a funny clicking noise.  So, i had the alternator removed, and it tested fine.  any ideas of what it could be?  maybe starter, or distributor?  Any thought would be great.  <BR>Kathrin.<p>[This message has been edited by BloodFlower (edited 02-17-2001).]||||reg||9
Z||000001||My7Fieros||02-17-2001||01:38 PM||||Hey! Man I used to live in Jacksonville, Al! Wierd, never thought Id see somebody else from there! My mom and brothers still live there..off Louise Dr.<BR>Ive never encountered a situation like that...could be the starter, or just a dead cell in the battery.<BR>Theres also a member on this forum that goes by the name of Cooter..he lives in Oxford...<BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://tgowens.com/My7Fieros_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phaeton||02-17-2001||01:58 PM||||Second guessing time.  If the alternator and battery really are ok a high draw short probably exists somewhere.<BR>Charge the battery and hook up the positive cable, put an amp meter between the negative terminal and cable, if the reading is over a couple hundred milliamps start pulling fuses until the draw quits and go from there.<P>Was the battery load tested or did the shop just put a voltmeter on it and call it good 'cause the unloaded voltage was twelve?<BR>My personal bet is on the battery.<P>That clicking is the starter solenoid, a big switch that puts power to the starter.  It clicks the switch shut but when the starter starts drawing current the battery cannot maintain the voltage and the switch pops open.  But without the starter drawing power the voltage is high enough to shut the switch again causing the voltage to drop and the switch to open and you end up with click click click.<BR>This circular logic makes me dizzy, what color is your Fiero?||||reg||12
Z||000003||theogre||02-17-2001||03:28 PM||||The charge(bat) light was on.... That can only be on if the alternator is showing a problem.<P>The alternator can test good and still be bad. It may not be getting a good connection to something inside. When you pulled it for testing you bumped and turned it while handling it. That can let whatever is wrong get a better connection for awhile.<P>If the 2 terminals on the side of the ALT were corroded then just the simple act of unplugging the connector and inserting the test system connector may have cleaned the terminals enough to make it work.<P>Being that the alternator is likely getting pretty old, it might not be a bad idea to replace it. If you don't replace it clean the terminals and grease them with "dielectric" grease to prevent them corroding again.||||reg||1
Z||000004||ToddAsh||02-17-2001||04:19 PM|||| I agree.I'd replace the alternator.These are the kind of problems where "mechanics" do diagnostic part replacing.Alternator,starter and battery. Good Luck.The battery went bad in my bike and it would start and run but would all of a sudden quit.I searched for a short until a Harley guy I work with said it was a battery short. That fixed it. Todd||||reg||1
Z||000005||G-Nasty||02-17-2001||04:20 PM||||The ogre speaks the truth-get a new alternator. Also get a 600+ crank DIE HARD Gold battery or equivalent. These cars burn and eat batteries for lunch.||||reg||1
